# module|page|term|definition
1|2|Master–apprentice system|Community-based transfer of building skills from an experienced craftsperson to a learner.
1|2|Maestro de Obras|Historical master builder title used during the Spanish period in the Philippines.
1|2|Académico|Academic route or title distinguished from the practical route in early Maestro de Obras training.
1|2|Práctico|Practical route or title distinguished from the academic route in early building training.
1|2|Félix Roxas y Arroyo|Architect described in the module as the first Filipino architect.
1|2|Ayuntamiento|Manila building listed among works associated with Félix Roxas y Arroyo.
1|2|AAAF|1902 Academia de Arquitectura y Agremensura de Filipinas, an early architects and surveyors organization.
1|3|AIAAF|1903 Academia de Arquitectura, Ingeniería y Agremensura de Filipinas, which included engineers, architects, and surveyors.
1|3|Tarifa de Honorarios|Early professional-practice and fee schedule promoted by the association in 1903.
1|3|Act No. 2985|1921 measure that separated architecture and civil-engineering examiner boards.
1|3|Philippine Architects' Society|Organization formed in 1933 with Juan Nakpil as president.
1|3|Philippine Institute of Architects|Postwar professional organization traced from PAS through PIAP.
1|3|League of Philippine Architects|Architect organization involved in later professional unification.
1|4|RA 544|1949 law governing civil engineering in the module's historical timeline.
1|4|RA 545|Earlier architecture law repealed by RA 9266.
1|4|APGA|One of the architect organizations that joined professional unification discussions.
1|4|PRC|Professional Regulation Commission, created under Presidential Decree No. 223.
1|5|UAP|United Architects of the Philippines, the profession's integrated and accredited organization in the module.
1|5|IAPOA|Integrated and Accredited Professional Organization of Architects.
1|5|RA 9266|Architecture Act of 2004, the central law governing architecture practice in the module.
1|6|PD 1096|National Building Code of the Philippines.
1|7|Architectural program|Organized account of a client's functions and space needs before design.
1|7|Pre-design services|Studies and advice undertaken before the regular design sequence.
1|7|Design-build|Service approach involving both architectural design and construction delivery.
1|7|Specialized allied services|Architectural expertise that supplements regular design, such as acoustics or lighting.
1|7|Comprehensive architectural services|Coordination of several service stages in a broader professional engagement.
1|8|Construction documents|Drawings and specifications that communicate the project for permits, pricing, and building.
2|2|CHED|Commission on Higher Education, which regulates higher-education programs including private BS Architecture programs.
2|2|PSG|Policies, Standards, and Guidelines for architecture education.
2|2|Outcomes-based education|Education organized around demonstrable competencies and learning results.
2|3|RLA|Registered and Licensed Architect.
2|3|Professional identification card|Current professional credential used with registration to demonstrate authority to practice.
2|3|Diversified architectural experience|Post-baccalaureate, pre-licensure work through varied phases of architectural service.
2|4|ALE|Architect Licensure Examination.
2|6|DT Form 001|Training summary form recording experience by field of practice.
2|6|DT Form 002|Mentor-completed form recording project experience and training hours.
2|6|FOP|Field of Practice, a category used to organize diversified training hours.
2|11|IRR|Implementing Rules and Regulations that give operational detail to RA 9266.
2|12|Reciprocity|Condition under which qualified foreign practitioners may be allowed comparable practice rights.
2|12|CPD|Continuing Professional Development undertaken after licensure.
2|14|Architect of Record|Architect responsible for identified design documents, including signing and sealing professional work.
2|14|Intellectual property|Ownership and authorship interests in professional work such as signed architectural documents.
2|16|Architectural firm|Registered business entity offering architectural services under ownership and registration conditions.
2|17|Special permit|Authorization process for a foreign architect's work on a Philippine project under stated conditions.
2|18|Professional liability insurance|Coverage for claims arising from an architect's professional errors or omissions.
2|20|Conflict of interest|Personal or financial interest that may impair objective professional judgment.
2|20|ADR|Alternative dispute resolution, such as mediation or arbitration before litigation.
2|21|Nonconforming work|Construction or materials that fail to meet approved drawings or specifications.
2|23|Written service agreement|Contract stating scope, responsibilities, fees, dispute resolution, and termination terms.
3|2|SPP|Standards of Professional Practice organizing architectural service categories and compensation.
3|3|PRBoA|Professional Regulatory Board of Architecture under the PRC.
3|3|AoR|Architect of Record, the architect responsible for the identified professional design work.
3|4|Terms of reference|Project brief or selection document setting scope and evaluation expectations.
3|5|SPP 201|Standard for pre-design services.
3|5|Pre-feasibility study|Early viability review based mainly on available secondary information.
3|5|Feasibility study|More developed investigation of project viability using primary information.
3|6|Architectural programming|Definition of user activities, relationships, and space needs.
3|6|Value management|Review of alternatives to improve the relationship between project function and cost.
3|7|SPP 202|Standard for regular design services.
3|7|Schematic design|Initial concepts, alternatives, and overall spatial or formal scheme.
3|8|Design development|Refinement and coordination of the approved schematic design.
3|8|Contract documents|Drawings and specifications prepared for approvals, pricing, and construction.
3|9|DAEDS|Design services under one owner-architect agreement with engineering and allied subconsultants coordinated by the architect.
3|9|DADS|Design service arrangement in which the owner contracts separately with allied professionals.
3|10|SPP 203|Standard for specialized architectural services.
3|11|Forensic architecture|Professional investigation of building defects or failures.
3|11|SPP 204A|Standard for full-time construction supervision services.
3|11|CSG|Construction Supervision Group that monitors and records site progress under its defined role.
3|12|SPP 204B|Standard for construction management services.
3|12|Construction manager|Professional coordinating project time, cost, quality, and records during construction.
3|13|SPP 205|Standard for post-construction services.
3|13|Post-occupancy evaluation|Assessment of how a building performs after users move in.
3|14|SPP 206|Standard for comprehensive architectural services.
3|15|Project manager|Professional coordinating project-wide time, cost, and quality responsibilities.
3|16|SPP 207|Standard for design-build services.
3|16|Guaranteed maximum cost|Design-build arrangement with an agreed upper construction-cost commitment and defined savings or excess treatment.
3|18|SPP 208|Standard for architectural design competitions.
3|18|Architectural design competition|Organized comparison of design proposals under a common brief and judging process.
3|18|Professional adviser|Person who helps organize an architectural design competition without voting as a juror.
3|22|SPP 209|Standard for professional architectural consulting services.
3|22|PACS|Professional Architectural Consulting Services.
3|23|SPP 210|Guidelines for architect compensation and professional fees.
3|23|RPF|Recommended Professional Fee stated by a professional-practice guideline.
3|23|Direct personnel expense|Staff cost used as a basis for multiplying fees to cover overhead and profit.
3|24|PCC|Project Construction Cost, a construction-cost basis used in some fee calculations.
3|24|PDC|Project Development Cost, a broader cost including PCC and additional project costs.
3|24|Reimbursable expense|Project expense repaid by the client under the service agreement.
3|30|Project group|SPP classification of projects by type or complexity for compensation guidelines.
